WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.105
&gt;&gt; While he's setting that up,

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:08.610
I can quickly introduce
our group as the rebel alliance.

00:00:08.611 --> 00:00:11.040
We weren't really
an officially sanctioned group,

00:00:11.041 --> 00:00:15.630
but all of us have
an interest in urban issues,

00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:16.800
urban quality of life issues,

00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:18.690
and getting city
administrators and

00:00:18.691 --> 00:00:21.970
municipalities to make
more citizen-centered decisions,

00:00:21.971 --> 00:00:25.300
and so we organically
came together.

00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:28.885
The folks represented in
this group include IIT Bombay,

00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:31.870
Ashoka University,
Microsoft Research,

00:00:31.871 --> 00:00:33.150
and Lakeer, which is

00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:35.800
an urban governance
nonprofit that I run.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.255
Why does it matter
to us so much that

00:00:40.256 --> 00:00:41.510
city administrators are making

00:00:41.511 --> 00:00:43.460
good quality decisions or not?

00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:44.990
The context is that
India has actually

00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:47.160
already become an urban country.

00:00:47.161 --> 00:00:48.140
This is not officially

00:00:48.141 --> 00:00:49.580
recognized there in
government records,

00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:51.165
but when you look
at satellite data,

00:00:51.166 --> 00:00:53.560
depending on how you cut
it, about 55 percent to

00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:55.070
78 percent of India is already

00:00:55.071 --> 00:00:57.000
urban and we're growing
very, very rapidly.

00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:00.860
1,800 Indians move to a city
every hour in search of

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:04.070
better opportunities
and often the quality

00:01:04.071 --> 00:01:05.855
of life that they
meet in these cities

00:01:05.856 --> 00:01:07.100
are not up to mark.

00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:08.780
That's because the folks who run

00:01:08.781 --> 00:01:10.885
these cities or
municipalities basically,

00:01:10.886 --> 00:01:13.390
they're under-resourced
and under-capacitated,

00:01:13.391 --> 00:01:17.705
and are naturally in a place
where they can make good,

00:01:17.706 --> 00:01:19.370
smart decisions about where to

00:01:19.371 --> 00:01:22.620
prioritize their limited budgets.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.680
So, common area of

00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:29.100
interests between the work

00:01:29.101 --> 00:01:30.315
that IIT Bombay has been doing,

00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:31.370
Ashoka University has been

00:01:31.371 --> 00:01:32.755
doing, and Lakeer has been doing,

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:35.030
is that data-driven
decision making can

00:01:35.031 --> 00:01:37.340
really improve
the current state of

00:01:37.341 --> 00:01:39.590
being off affairs and lead to

00:01:39.591 --> 00:01:43.070
more equitable quality of life.

00:01:43.071 --> 00:01:47.340
But the question is
really, where is the data?

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:50.390
All of us were
inspired by the work

00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:52.910
that Professor Ganesh
presented yesterday talking

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:54.650
about grievance data because it

00:01:54.651 --> 00:01:56.705
then struck as that
actually grievance data is

00:01:56.706 --> 00:01:58.685
some data that's available across

00:01:58.686 --> 00:02:00.980
all urban areas in India,

00:02:00.981 --> 00:02:03.535
especially with populations
more than 25,000.

00:02:03.536 --> 00:02:05.330
This data is somewhat real-time.

00:02:05.331 --> 00:02:07.200
It's collected by
government agencies,

00:02:07.201 --> 00:02:09.390
as well as by the private sector,

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:12.800
and it's refreshed
at a regular rate,

00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:14.600
and it's also available
back in the past,

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:17.145
going back at least
three, four years.

00:02:17.146 --> 00:02:19.470
What's unfortunate is that today,

00:02:19.471 --> 00:02:22.325
this data is basically
used as a to-do list.

00:02:22.326 --> 00:02:25.205
So, I can show you
what it looks like.

00:02:25.206 --> 00:02:29.210
This is all of the grievances
received by the city of

00:02:29.211 --> 00:02:32.840
Hyderabad in the last one
month and basically the way

00:02:32.841 --> 00:02:35.530
that the city uses
this data is in

00:02:35.531 --> 00:02:37.430
each individual point as

00:02:37.431 --> 00:02:39.800
one item to mark off on
a to-do list saying,

00:02:39.801 --> 00:02:41.585
"This complaint came
in and we acted on it,

00:02:41.586 --> 00:02:44.730
there's nothing else
done beyond that."

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.270
The questions we started asking

00:02:47.271 --> 00:02:49.640
ourselves in the group
yesterday is,

00:02:49.641 --> 00:02:51.080
what if we could do
more with this data?

00:02:51.081 --> 00:02:54.515
What if we could analyze
this data to figure out

00:02:54.516 --> 00:02:56.780
where to prioritize our resources

00:02:56.781 --> 00:02:58.420
in a little more intelligent way?

00:02:58.421 --> 00:03:00.140
To give you an example
of why that is

00:03:00.141 --> 00:03:02.295
needed for a city like Hyderabad,

00:03:02.296 --> 00:03:04.820
the annual municipal budget per

00:03:04.821 --> 00:03:07.405
citizen per year is
only 800 rupees.

00:03:07.406 --> 00:03:09.440
So, exactly equal distribution

00:03:09.441 --> 00:03:11.270
of this money will get
us exactly nowhere,

00:03:11.271 --> 00:03:13.765
so that prioritization is needed.

00:03:13.766 --> 00:03:15.800
The second, what if you
could cross tabulate

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:19.010
this grievance data with
other datasets like budgets,

00:03:19.011 --> 00:03:20.420
like tenders to figure
out if there are

00:03:20.421 --> 00:03:21.900
some underlying patterns to why

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.365
these grievances are coming
up in the first place,

00:03:24.366 --> 00:03:26.450
and therefore maybe prevent them

00:03:26.451 --> 00:03:28.915
from happening with
better urban planning.

00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:32.105
The third is, what
if we could predict

00:03:32.106 --> 00:03:34.420
hotspots in spaces as

00:03:34.421 --> 00:03:35.810
well as in time of
where grievances

00:03:35.811 --> 00:03:36.820
are more likely to occur,

00:03:36.821 --> 00:03:38.930
so that the limited number of

00:03:38.931 --> 00:03:40.390
government personnel that do

00:03:40.391 --> 00:03:43.230
exist can be deployed
more effectively.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.940
But there are barriers
to getting there.

00:03:46.941 --> 00:03:48.940
The first, and a lot

00:03:48.941 --> 00:03:50.330
of these Professor
Ganesh spoke about,

00:03:50.331 --> 00:03:52.040
so I'm going to go
through this quickly.

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:54.515
So, data is not
properly geolocated.

00:03:54.516 --> 00:03:56.285
It is of inconsistent quality,

00:03:56.286 --> 00:03:58.010
people use different words

00:03:58.011 --> 00:03:59.685
from across different languages,

00:03:59.686 --> 00:04:02.520
different sentiments to
describe the same problem.

00:04:02.521 --> 00:04:04.635
The data is not inter-linked.

00:04:04.636 --> 00:04:06.470
So, in the same city,
grievance data will

00:04:06.471 --> 00:04:08.790
be reported to multiple
government agencies,

00:04:08.791 --> 00:04:10.580
multiple private
agencies, and there's

00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:13.580
no one common place where
all of this comes and rests.

00:04:13.581 --> 00:04:15.320
Social media data is

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.900
not being collated even
though there are a lot

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:19.655
of government agencies
in India now that

00:04:19.656 --> 00:04:21.730
actively respond to
complaints on Twitter.

00:04:21.731 --> 00:04:23.000
Many of you will be familiar

00:04:23.001 --> 00:04:26.480
with the Mumbai police is
a good example of that.

00:04:26.481 --> 00:04:29.515
Big, big ethical problem.

00:04:29.516 --> 00:04:33.500
Grievance data relies on
people to have agency to

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:35.375
report whether it's through

00:04:35.376 --> 00:04:37.250
the web or through phone calls,

00:04:37.251 --> 00:04:39.860
and that tends to be
predominantly in areas that are

00:04:39.861 --> 00:04:42.410
better off versus
low-income location.

00:04:42.411 --> 00:04:44.335
So, there's a huge bias problem.

00:04:44.336 --> 00:04:46.040
Then finally, multi-modal data

00:04:46.041 --> 00:04:47.730
analysis is not done because,

00:04:47.731 --> 00:04:48.990
for instance, in Hyderabad,

00:04:48.991 --> 00:04:51.590
about 40 percent of
the complaints that come in,

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:54.320
come in via phone, and

00:04:54.321 --> 00:04:57.570
that speech was not
analyzed separately.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.000
So, where does AI come in?

00:05:00.001 --> 00:05:01.400
Again, Professor Ganesh spoke

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:02.660
about some of this when it comes

00:05:02.661 --> 00:05:06.484
to augmenting and organizing
the grievance database.

00:05:06.485 --> 00:05:10.910
So, understanding the complaints,

00:05:10.911 --> 00:05:15.930
separating signals from
noise is one potential area.

00:05:15.931 --> 00:05:17.090
Just to give you a scale of

00:05:17.091 --> 00:05:18.830
the number of complaints
that annually come

00:05:18.831 --> 00:05:21.810
in for a city such as
Bangalore or Hyderabad,

00:05:21.811 --> 00:05:23.180
we're looking at
annually about half

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:25.330
a million individual complaints.

00:05:25.331 --> 00:05:29.830
So, this is not something
that can be manually done.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:32.910
There is the problem of

00:05:32.911 --> 00:05:34.070
figuring out what people are

00:05:34.071 --> 00:05:35.860
saying versus what
they actually mean.

00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:37.915
We saw this example yesterday,

00:05:37.916 --> 00:05:40.310
when Professor described
the problem of cows

00:05:40.311 --> 00:05:42.995
on the road versus how
people actually reported.

00:05:42.996 --> 00:05:44.645
There's a problem of a lot of

00:05:44.646 --> 00:05:46.070
vernacular data that's typed in

00:05:46.071 --> 00:05:49.785
English and then
can't be analyzed.

00:05:49.786 --> 00:05:55.280
There's a lot of analysis
that goes missing in terms

00:05:55.281 --> 00:05:57.470
of which entity
the problem is being

00:05:57.471 --> 00:05:58.970
reported about and what

00:05:58.971 --> 00:06:00.130
is actually the nature
of the problem,

00:06:00.131 --> 00:06:03.210
summarization of the
data that's coming in.

00:06:03.211 --> 00:06:06.545
The other issue that I could

00:06:06.546 --> 00:06:08.030
potentially help with is give us

00:06:08.031 --> 00:06:10.145
richer insights
through cross-tabbing.

00:06:10.146 --> 00:06:11.700
This is an example of that.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:14.950
So, three different datasets
have been combined here.

00:06:14.951 --> 00:06:16.880
So, the red dots that you see are

00:06:16.881 --> 00:06:21.360
individual grievances of
water logging when it rains.

00:06:21.361 --> 00:06:24.440
The terrain map along with
these streams is basically

00:06:24.441 --> 00:06:26.240
a watershed analysis
to show what parts

00:06:26.241 --> 00:06:28.475
of the city are likely
to flood when it rains.

00:06:28.476 --> 00:06:30.680
The black dots that
you see are basically

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:32.870
rehabilitation centers
that have been identified

00:06:32.871 --> 00:06:34.880
for this city for where citizens

00:06:34.881 --> 00:06:37.660
should be relocated
if there is flooding.

00:06:37.661 --> 00:06:39.680
One thing that crops
up is a lot of

00:06:39.681 --> 00:06:41.210
the rehabilitation
centers are actually

00:06:41.211 --> 00:06:43.245
located in areas, which
are going to flood.

00:06:43.246 --> 00:06:45.585
So, insights like these come

00:06:45.586 --> 00:06:48.510
out more obviously when there
is cross-tabbing of data.

00:06:48.511 --> 00:06:50.925
We see huge potential
to, for example,

00:06:50.926 --> 00:06:53.710
bring in a lot of information
from OpenStreetMaps,

00:06:53.711 --> 00:06:55.760
layer this on top of
the grievance data,

00:06:55.761 --> 00:06:58.140
and find such patterns.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:01.370
So, the project that
we would like to scope

00:07:01.371 --> 00:07:04.800
out and it's still
very much exploratory,

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:07.370
it's not as concrete as
some of the other teams

00:07:07.371 --> 00:07:10.190
that have presented
because like I said,

00:07:10.191 --> 00:07:11.785
we came together
quite organically.

00:07:11.786 --> 00:07:13.790
But basically, in
the first three months,

00:07:13.791 --> 00:07:17.120
we want to focus on developing
base maps for urban areas.

00:07:17.121 --> 00:07:19.820
So, get some common datasets

00:07:19.821 --> 00:07:21.520
in place in terms of
the jurisdictions,

00:07:21.521 --> 00:07:25.090
roads, boundaries, what
are the public assets.

00:07:25.091 --> 00:07:26.870
We want to develop some standards

00:07:26.871 --> 00:07:28.100
around geolocations that can

00:07:28.101 --> 00:07:29.720
be adapted across the partners

00:07:29.721 --> 00:07:31.100
that at least we
are working with.

00:07:31.101 --> 00:07:33.000
So, for example, between us,

00:07:33.001 --> 00:07:36.100
we're working with
four city municipalities and

00:07:36.101 --> 00:07:38.249
three private sector
organizations

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.815
that's saw our grievance data.

00:07:39.816 --> 00:07:43.455
Automate the data categorization,
and summarization,

00:07:43.456 --> 00:07:45.350
and begin predicting what are

00:07:45.351 --> 00:07:48.215
likely hotspots of grievances.

00:07:48.216 --> 00:07:50.070
From there, what we want to do

00:07:50.071 --> 00:07:52.505
is move onto
cross-tabbing this data

00:07:52.506 --> 00:07:55.100
with other potential datasets

00:07:55.101 --> 00:07:57.470
like jurisdictions,
budgets, standards.

00:07:57.471 --> 00:08:00.260
One question that we
ask in our group is,

00:08:00.261 --> 00:08:01.970
what if we could take
the potholes database,

00:08:01.971 --> 00:08:03.925
put it on top of
the tenders database,

00:08:03.926 --> 00:08:05.720
and find a link between

00:08:05.721 --> 00:08:07.340
the contractors who are building

00:08:07.341 --> 00:08:09.230
the roads that have
more potholes again and again,

00:08:09.231 --> 00:08:11.085
and so they could be penalized?

00:08:11.086 --> 00:08:12.980
Figure it out if there's a way to

00:08:12.981 --> 00:08:15.200
do more intelligent
prioritization

00:08:15.201 --> 00:08:19.280
of the geographies of focus
and the issues of focus.

00:08:19.281 --> 00:08:22.180
Finally, in the last six months,

00:08:22.181 --> 00:08:24.200
get two multi-modal analysis

00:08:24.201 --> 00:08:25.735
across multiple data sources.

00:08:25.736 --> 00:08:27.110
So, not just text,

00:08:27.111 --> 00:08:28.625
but also image and audio,

00:08:28.626 --> 00:08:31.610
and look if it's possible to do

00:08:31.611 --> 00:08:34.570
some decentralized action
around grievances.

00:08:34.571 --> 00:08:36.170
So, not everything needs to

00:08:36.171 --> 00:08:38.805
necessarily read out back to
the government for action.

00:08:38.806 --> 00:08:41.350
Some things could come to
civil society organizations,

00:08:41.351 --> 00:08:45.050
some things could go
to citizen groups.

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:48.650
Because we didn't have an NGO
at the center of it all,

00:08:48.651 --> 00:08:50.780
we had to come up with who

00:08:50.781 --> 00:08:52.975
the potential implementation
partners are.

00:08:52.976 --> 00:08:54.680
So, what we're thinking
right now is that

00:08:54.681 --> 00:08:56.855
IIT Bombay will take a lead on

00:08:56.856 --> 00:08:59.270
automating the grievance database

00:08:59.271 --> 00:09:01.550
augmentation and organization,

00:09:01.551 --> 00:09:03.160
and from that surfacing,

00:09:03.161 --> 00:09:05.170
what are some
actionable insights.

00:09:05.171 --> 00:09:07.135
What we looked at Lakeer is

00:09:07.136 --> 00:09:09.800
get focused on cross-tabbing
this grievance data

00:09:09.801 --> 00:09:12.170
with other datasets
that could be scrapped

00:09:12.171 --> 00:09:15.170
or RTI-ed from the government.

00:09:15.171 --> 00:09:18.920
Ashoka University will
basically focus more on

00:09:18.921 --> 00:09:21.260
municipal works and
testing products

00:09:21.261 --> 00:09:23.230
through the chief ministers,

00:09:23.231 --> 00:09:25.965
good governance,
associate fellows.

00:09:25.966 --> 00:09:28.700
The test I've said that
prioritizing for this are

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:32.235
the municipalities in Mumbai,
Bangalore, and Hyderabad.

00:09:32.236 --> 00:09:35.490
That brings me to the end
of the presentation.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:42.560
&gt;&gt; So, for the multi-modal data,

00:09:42.561 --> 00:09:44.840
what specifically with the data

00:09:44.841 --> 00:09:48.300
which comes over the phone?

00:09:48.301 --> 00:09:51.830
Why not have a human
sitting over there?

00:09:51.831 --> 00:09:54.380
[inaudible] interface to tag

00:09:54.381 --> 00:09:57.770
that data and then why
you need AI for it?

00:09:57.771 --> 00:09:59.415
&gt;&gt; Yes. It's a great question.

00:09:59.416 --> 00:10:00.960
I'll also let Professor
Ganesh to comment.

00:10:00.961 --> 00:10:02.570
So, the government
data that comes in,

00:10:02.571 --> 00:10:04.270
that's how it works.
There's a call center.

00:10:04.271 --> 00:10:04.900
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:10:04.901 --> 00:10:05.800
&gt;&gt; I think the private
sector guys may

00:10:05.801 --> 00:10:06.610
not be able to afford it.

00:10:06.611 --> 00:10:10.960
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:10:10.961 --> 00:10:13.305
Besides the point of not
being able to afford,

00:10:13.306 --> 00:10:16.020
I think even if some AIs
used to cleanup

00:10:16.021 --> 00:10:18.980
the audio that comes

00:10:18.981 --> 00:10:22.280
in and basically bootstrap
around the user feedback,

00:10:22.281 --> 00:10:25.805
were there repetitive
corrections that are made by

00:10:25.806 --> 00:10:28.730
the repetitive patterns that

00:10:28.731 --> 00:10:32.070
emerge in the process of
transcription by a human,

00:10:32.071 --> 00:10:34.285
can that release automated by,

00:10:34.286 --> 00:10:36.450
and this is our of experience
with this project,

00:10:36.451 --> 00:10:38.565
it's IIT Bombay Call
Spoken Tutorial.

00:10:38.566 --> 00:10:42.290
Now, there's a lot of
speech data and text.

00:10:42.291 --> 00:10:46.115
We realized that the very
common accentuated patterns

00:10:46.116 --> 00:10:48.045
that could be actually
captured through AI.

00:10:48.046 --> 00:10:49.470
So, I agree and most

00:10:49.471 --> 00:10:51.660
reliable thing we'll
do, we'll get a human.

00:10:51.661 --> 00:10:55.725
But can we get an AI
to assist the human.

00:10:55.726 --> 00:10:58.610
&gt;&gt; There was also
another project by

00:10:58.611 --> 00:11:01.350
IBM called Spoken Web that also

00:11:01.351 --> 00:11:04.905
did tag certain texts

00:11:04.906 --> 00:11:07.940
from the verbal calls
that had made.

00:11:07.941 --> 00:11:09.485
I'm not sure whether
you guys are aware of,

00:11:09.486 --> 00:11:12.140
but it allowed you
to search through

00:11:12.141 --> 00:11:14.540
a particular word and then

00:11:14.541 --> 00:11:17.035
you can search
the whole voice database.

00:11:17.036 --> 00:11:18.800
Unfortunately,
that project is over,

00:11:18.801 --> 00:11:21.040
I don't know whether IBM
is actually taking it.

00:11:21.041 --> 00:11:23.345
&gt;&gt; It began when I
left IBM research.

00:11:23.346 --> 00:11:25.580
&gt;&gt; Okay. I know

00:11:25.581 --> 00:11:29.670
Nintendra and all working
on to it, so yes.

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.790
&gt;&gt; I was just saying that we
see this as a phase two of

00:11:44.791 --> 00:11:47.070
vendor for all of the things
we've been talking about.

